# Chapter 8 · The Impact of Women Representatives: Do Women Make a Difference?

> The syllabus names "**impact of women representatives on politics**". The 2017 question quoted: "Women's active political participation could **stabilise democracy** and **boost economic development**." The 2018 question asked whether we need **equal numbers** of women in politics ("women cover half the sky"), and the 2021 question asked whether the quota is "**showing results**".

## 8.1  How to measure impact

"Impact" can mean several things. A good answer separates them:

::: fig grid | Four kinds of impact women representatives can have.
@cols 2
Policy impact: laws, budgets and programmes that address women's needs
Process impact: how politics is done — debate, consensus, conduct, attention to constituents
Symbolic impact: changed ideas about women's capacity; role models for girls
Democratic impact: legitimacy, trust, inclusion and stability of the system
:::

## 8.2  Evidence from around the world

::: case Women leaders in Indian village councils | Chattopadhyay and Duflo (2004)
India's ==1992== constitutional amendments reserved one-third of village council (*panchayat*) headships for women, chosen at **random** — a natural experiment. Economists **Raghabendra Chattopadhyay** and **Esther Duflo** studied villages in West Bengal and Rajasthan and found that women leaders invested more in public goods that **women** had asked for — especially **drinking water** — and in roads in West Bengal, where women had requested them.
-> Strong evidence that **who** governs changes **what** is provided.
:::

::: case Role models in India | Beaman and colleagues (2009, 2012)
Later studies in West Bengal found that after villages had women leaders, voters' **bias** against women leaders fell, and parents' **aspirations** for their daughters — and girls' own aspirations for education and careers — rose.
-> The **symbolic** impact of women in office is measurable.
:::

Other studies have linked more women legislators to greater attention to **health, education, childcare and violence against women**, and to lower tolerance of corruption in some contexts — although the evidence on corruption is mixed and depends on the political system.

## 8.3  Evidence from Pakistan

### (1) Laws for women

The clearest evidence of impact is the series of laws passed after ==2002==, many proposed or pushed by women legislators and the **Women's Parliamentary Caucus**:

::: table | Pro-women laws passed after the 2002 quota.
@widths 2,7,5
Year | Law | Role of women legislators
2006 | Protection of Women (Criminal Laws Amendment) Act | Debated in an assembly with 60+ women members
2010 | Protection against Harassment of Women at the Workplace Act; s. 509 PPC | Caucus and women members drove it
2011 | Acid Control and Acid Crime Prevention Act | Private member's bill by women MNAs
2011 | Prevention of Anti-Women Practices Act | Private member's bill by women MNAs
2012 | National Commission on the Status of Women Act | Caucus priority
2016 | Anti-honour-killing and anti-rape laws | Pushed by women senators and MNAs after Qandeel Baloch
2013–2021 | Provincial domestic-violence laws (Sindh, Balochistan, Punjab, KP) | Women members in provincial caucuses
:::

### (2) Parliamentary activity

Monitoring by **FAFEN** and **PILDAT** has repeatedly found women legislators — though only about one-fifth of members — among the most **active** in moving questions, calling-attention notices, resolutions and private members' bills, and with good **attendance**.

### (3) Agenda change

Issues once treated as "private" — domestic violence, harassment, acid attacks, honour killings, inheritance — have become regular business of the assemblies. Women members have also raised **health**, **education** and **social protection** — the **Benazir Income Support Programme** (==2008==), paid to women, was championed by women in the PPP government.

### (4) Local government

Studies of the women councillors of ==2001–2005== found mixed results. Many were **proxies**, and male councillors often excluded them from development funds. But many others raised women's concerns — water, girls' schools, health — and gained confidence and respect; some moved on to provincial politics.

## 8.3a  A case: how the 2016 honour-killing law was passed

The story of the anti-honour-killing law shows women legislators as **critical actors**. Senator **Sughra Imam** moved an anti-honour-killing bill in ==2014==; the Senate passed it in ==2015==, but it stalled in the National Assembly. After the social-media celebrity **Qandeel Baloch** was murdered by her brother in **July 2016**, women legislators, the caucus, civil society and the media pressed again. A **joint sitting** of Parliament passed the Criminal Law (Amendment) (Offences in the name or pretext of Honour) Act in **October ==2016==**, making a life sentence mandatory even if the family forgives the killer. The law did not end honour killings, but it closed the "forgiveness" loophole that had let many killers go free.

## 8.4  Women, democracy and development (2017)

### (1) Stabilising democracy

- **Legitimacy:** a parliament that includes women is more representative and more trusted.
- **Peace and consensus:** research on peace processes (linked to **UN Security Council Resolution 1325**, ==2000==) suggests agreements are more durable when women take part. Women legislators in Pakistan have often worked **across party lines** — the caucus is one of the few cross-party bodies in a polarised parliament.
- **Inclusion:** bringing half the population into politics widens the base of democracy and reduces alienation.

### (2) Boosting economic development

- **Better spending:** women representatives tend to favour **health, education, water and nutrition** — investments with high returns (the Chattopadhyay–Duflo evidence).
- **Women's economic rights:** laws on harassment, property (==2020==), home-based workers (Sindh ==2018==) and maternity leave help women enter and stay in work — and raising female labour-force participation from ==24.4%== would add greatly to GDP.
- **Human capital:** girls' education and maternal health, often championed by women, raise productivity in the next generation.

::: fig cycle | How women's political participation can strengthen democracy and development.
@centre Women in politics
More inclusive, legitimate democracy
Laws and budgets for health, education, water
Protection of women's rights and work
More women in paid work and higher incomes
Educated, healthy next generation
Greater demand for women's voice
:::

### (3) Within Pakistan's socio-economic realities

The 2017 question asked you to comment "within the context of the **socio-economic realities** of Pakistani society". The realities that **limit** this potential are: **poverty** and **low literacy**; **patriarchal norms**; **feudal and tribal** power; **dynastic** politics; **military interventions** that interrupted democracy; and women legislators' **dependence on party leaders**. A balanced answer accepts the statement's logic but shows that women's participation can stabilise democracy and boost development only if it is **real** — independent, resourced and supported.

## 8.5  Do we need equal numbers? (2018)

The Chinese saying "**women hold up half the sky**" (associated with **Mao Zedong**) expresses the idea that women are equal contributors to society.

::: balance | Equal numbers of women in all public spheres?
- **For parity:** justice — half the citizens should hold half the power; descriptive representation; women's distinct interests; evidence of policy impact; **parity laws** in France (==2000==) and many Latin American countries, and **Rwanda**'s majority-women parliament, show it can be done.
- **Against rigid parity:** individuals should be chosen on merit and choice; women are not one group with one interest; forced parity may produce proxies; some women may not wish to enter politics because of care duties.
- **A middle view:** aim at **parity of opportunity** and remove barriers; use quotas as a **means** towards a critical mass (30%) and eventually parity; judge success by **outcomes**, not numbers alone.
-> **Line to take:** the goal of **equal participation** is right — and is the logic of CEDAW and SDG target **5.5** — but numbers must come with real power and independence.
:::

## 8.6  Limits of impact — why presence does not always mean power

1. **Party discipline** — members vote with the party; women's issues compete with party priorities.
2. **Dependence** — reserved-seat women owe their seats to leaders.
3. **Elite composition** — many women legislators are from privileged families and may not represent poor women.
4. **Marginal posts** — few women control finance, interior or planning.
5. **Implementation gap** — laws passed are poorly enforced (Topic 5).
6. **Backlash** — conservative members and bodies such as the Council of Islamic Ideology have opposed some laws (the Punjab violence law of ==2016==).

::: eye | Using this chapter
For "Is the quota showing results?" (2021), show **impact in four areas** (policy, process, symbolic, democratic) with Pakistani evidence (the laws table; the caucus; activity data), then the **limits**, then reforms. For 2017, use the **cycle figure** and the **socio-economic realities**.
:::
